add('def-qc-unbounded-error-time-space-counting-model','definition','QC23','Use fixed multitape fair-bit probabilistic machines with read-only uncharged input, charged work cells, worst-case halting bounds on every branch and an explicit random-access versus sequential simulation convention. PrTIME(t) and PrTISP(t,s) mean acceptance greater than one-half on YES and at most one-half on NO with time O(t) and work space O(s). Exact ties can be normalized with an extra bounded coin gadget before complementing. For oracle A, PrTIME^A(t) charges writing query strings and supplies exact classical oracle answers. Define LCH1=PrTIME(n), LCH_(k+1)=PrTIME(n)^(LCH_k). Majority formulas use at-least-half assignments; strict versus nonstrict majority conventions require an explicit threshold adjustment.', ['def-probabilistic-polynomial-time-machine-interface','def-effective-binary-encoding-and-decoder'], ['State clocked branch resource and oracle tape rules; finite fair bits give dyadic probabilities. Prove strict/nonstrict majority conversion by a biased acceptance offset smaller than the least nonzero dyadic gap and explicit extra coin bits. Root PTM definition is reused for transition semantics, not for unbounded-error time-space theorems.'],u,'Section2, PDFpp3–4; machine/linear-time counting hierarchy conventions')
add('thm-qc-probabilistic-polynomial-time-hierarchy','theorem','QC23','For rational 0<a<b with time-constructible polynomial bounds, PrTIME(n^a) is properly contained in PrTIME(n^b) in the declared uniform unbounded-error probabilistic machine model.',['def-qc-unbounded-error-time-space-counting-model'],['Use the source time-hierarchy diagonalization: enumerate encoded clocked machines, simulate the selected one within n^a polylog n time, normalize its possible tie by a dyadic offset below its minimum nonzero branch probability, and complement its threshold decision by swapping accepting/rejecting outcomes with the offset gadget.', 'Padding permits infinitely many encodings of each fixed program and absorbs the simulation constant. The universal overhead is below n^b for large n; finite exceptions are hardcoded. Author exact clock, tie and encoding bounds rather than importing nondeterministic hierarchy as a probabilistic theorem.'],u,'Theorem2, PDFp4; polynomial-exponent slack variant used by Theorem4')
add('thm-qc-uniform-threshold-iterated-integer-arithmetic','theorem','QC23','For m≥1 binary nonnegative w-bit integers, iterated sum and product admit constant-depth polynomial-size uniform threshold circuits over AND/OR/NOT/MAJ with explicit binary output lengths O(w+log m) and mw, respectively; use the source logspace-uniform multiplication and DLOGTIME-uniform addition conventions, or a fully proved stronger DLOGTIME multiplication variant.',['def-effective-binary-encoding-and-decoder'],['Local authoring must construct uniform iterated addition/multiplication circuits and their correctness/size/depth/connection predicate bounds; this is a substantial supplier, not a consequence of ordinary schoolbook multiplication.', 'Allender Section3.1 Step2 cites Chiu–Davida–Litow and Hesse/Allender–Barrington–Hesse for multiplication. Those independent secondary papers have not been retrieved/read by this auditor; the exact local supplier remains an authoring/source hold.'],u,'Section3.1 Step2, PDFp8; cited uniform arithmetic supplier, independent full proof not read')
add('lem-qc-counting-time-space-to-linear-hierarchy','lemma','QC23','For constants alpha>1 and 0<beta<1 there exists finite integer tau(alpha,beta) with PrTISP(n^alpha,n^(1−beta)) contained in LCH_tau.',['def-qc-unbounded-error-time-space-counting-model','thm-qc-uniform-threshold-iterated-integer-arithmetic'],['Clock/time-stamp configurations, of count at most2^O(n^(1−beta)); subdivide a length n^alpha path into n^beta segments and sum over checkpoint tuples, multiplying subpath counts. Repeat a ceiling number of subdivisions until segment length is bounded; round powers/segments explicitly and use zero-padding for short end segments.', 'This produces constant-depth arithmetic count circuits of2^O(n) gates and polynomial output bit length. Substitute the uniform arithmetic supplier. Gate adjacency has an LCH connection predicate; if only logspace multiplication uniformity is supplied, author the finite alternating/linear-counting evaluation of its polynomial-log-length predicates. Evaluate each threshold layer by random gate guessing with unbiased off-fanin branches and exact majority threshold adjustment, giving finite tau. Every simulation/table/bit bound is local authoring work.'],u,'Lemma5 and Section3.1, PDFpp5–9')
add('lem-qc-tight-majority-formula-completeness','lemma','QC23','Every LCH1 language reduces to MajSAT, and every LCH2 language reduces to MajMajSAT, with output length n log^O(1)n and each output bit computable in log^O(1)n time and O(log n) space, in the stated input/machine/formula encodings.',['def-qc-unbounded-error-time-space-counting-model','thm-pp-is-sign-testing-gap-p','prop-sharpp-is-closed-under-sum-and-product'],['For LCH2 first collapse adaptive oracle calls into one final query using a unique witness for the accepting-path rank at the one-half threshold, as Section3.2 does. Author the exact C=TIME conjunction and rank sign-testing construction with linear resource bounds; generic PP closure alone does not supply those bounds.', 'Obliviously simulate the linear-time computation with n log n time, encode its deterministic trace by a unique-witness formula of n polylog n size, and use the source auxiliary-selector variable to center counts at exactly half. Apply this inside the outer majority for the two-block formula. Provide actual bit-index generation and logarithmic workspace for the reduction; their uniformity, uniqueness, threshold and length proofs cannot be replaced by ordinary polynomial NP completeness.'],u,'Section3.2, PDFpp10–12; source Fischer–Pippenger/Cook/GAP closure adaptations remain local obligations')
add('thm-qc-unbounded-error-counting-time-space-alternative','theorem','QC23','For every real d and epsilon>0 there exists c>1 such that either MajMajSAT is not in PrTIME(n^c), or MajSAT is not in PrTISP(n^d,n^(1−epsilon)), with exact majority and resource conventions as defined on this page.',['def-qc-unbounded-error-time-space-counting-model','thm-qc-probabilistic-polynomial-time-hierarchy','lem-qc-counting-time-space-to-linear-hierarchy','lem-qc-tight-majority-formula-completeness'],['Allender Theorem4: for 0<e<1 and a>1, assuming PrTIME(n) contained in PrTISP(n^a,n^e), choose b>1 with be<1. Padding then puts PrTIME(n^b) in PrTISP(n^(ba),n^(be)), hence LCH_tau by counting speedup. If LCH2 were in PrTIME(n^c), recursive oracle padding collapses LCH_tau into PrTIME(n^(c^(tau−1))). Choose rational1<c<b^(1/(tau−1)), contradicting the polynomial hierarchy supplier; enlarge tau to≥2 if necessary.', 'Transfer assumed MajSAT/MajMajSAT algorithms through the tight formula reductions, shrinking epsilon and increasing d by fixed exponent slack to absorb polylog factors. Pick a>max(1,d) and 0<e<1 appropriately; choose the final c>1 below the class-level exponent so polylog reduction factors are absorbed. Larger epsilon/negative d only strengthen the denied space/time claim; handle finite lengths separately. This derives VMW Lemma1 with its exact quantifiers.'],u,'Theorem4 and its proof, PDFpp5–6; Lemma6 padding; Section3.2, pp10–12; VMW Lemma1 reformulation')
edit('thm-qc-counting-hierarchy-time-space-lower-bound',deps=['thm-qc-unbounded-error-counting-time-space-alternative'],s=by['thm-qc-counting-hierarchy-time-space-lower-bound']['statement_or_definition'].replace('until its Allender supplier is locally supplied','until the now-explicit Allender prerequisite decomposition is locally authored and reviewed'),proof=['Apply the QC23 quantum-to-unbounded-probabilistic time/space simulation to hypothetical algorithms for both majority languages. Preserve each time/space exponent with a smaller fixed epsilon and a slightly enlarged fixed d to absorb polylog overhead, contradicting the explicit Allender alternative.', 'For the quasilinear MajMajSAT corollary, such an algorithm also solves MajSAT by a dummy majority block, in polynomial time and the same sublinear-space exponent. Choose the alternative c>1 and absorb n^(1+o(1)) into n^c. All source model, finite-bit simulation and prerequisite proofs are mandatory.'])
